    contentEn: `<h2>Getting from Bilbao Airport (BIO) to Zarautz</h2>
<p>The most direct answer to "how do I get from Bilbao Airport to Zarautz" is a private transfer: about 85 kilometres covered in roughly 1 hour and 5 minutes along the AP-8 motorway, door to door, with no transfers and no shared shuttle to wait for. Zarautz has no airport of its own, and while San Sebastián's small EAS airport sits a little closer geographically, its schedule is limited to a handful of daily flights on a short list of routes. Bilbao Airport (BIO), with its far broader network of destinations and airlines, including many more international connections, is the realistic arrival point for the great majority of visitors heading to Zarautz, whether they are arriving for a surf trip, a beach holiday, or a stop on a longer tour of the Basque coast.</p>
<p>Once you land at BIO, a private driver is waiting in the arrivals hall with a name board, already tracking your flight so a delayed landing never leaves you standing around wondering whether anyone is coming. From Loiu, the route runs east along the AP-8 coastal motorway, the same road that continues on towards San Sebastián, before an exit drops you down into Zarautz's town centre and seafront. It's a largely motorway drive rather than a winding coastal crawl, which keeps the journey holding fairly steady at around 65 minutes regardless of time of day, with only modest variation for weekend beach traffic during the busiest summer weeks, when Zarautz's population swells with holidaymakers and surfers drawn to its sand.</p>
<p>You can arrange your pickup point, flight number, and a fixed price in advance through <a href="/get-a-quote/">get a quote</a>, and check live arrival information for your flight at <a href="https://www.aena.es">aena.es</a> before you travel. The same private service runs in reverse for your return journey to the airport, and if your trip also touches other towns along the Basque coast or further into Spain, our city-to-city transfers service can cover those legs too, booked as a single, predictable itinerary rather than a string of separate arrangements made on arrival.</p>
<p>Surfers travelling with boards, families needing extra space for young children, and groups arriving for a beach weekend should mention their requirements when booking, so the right vehicle is waiting rather than improvised at the kerb. This matters more on an 85-kilometre transfer than it does on a short hop across town, since a car that's too small for boards, luggage, or a larger party turns a straightforward ride into a real inconvenience. Our <a href="/group-event-transfers/">group event transfers</a> service is built for exactly this kind of larger or equipment-heavy party heading to the coast.</p>
<h3>About Bilbao Airport (BIO)</h3>
<p>Bilbao Airport (BIO) is a single-terminal airport in the municipality of Loiu, a short drive from Bilbao city centre. Its terminal building, designed by the Valencian architect Santiago Calatrava, is known locally by its nickname, La Paloma — "the Dove" — for the way its curved, wing-like roof appears to lift off the ground. As the Basque Country's busiest airport by a considerable margin, it's the natural gateway not only for Bilbao itself but for the string of coastal towns further east, Zarautz very much included, that see relatively little direct international air traffic of their own.</p>
<h2>About Zarautz</h2>
<p>Zarautz is the largest town along the Basque coast west of San Sebastián, and the first thing most visitors notice is its beach, which stretches for roughly 2.5 kilometres in a long, gentle curve that makes it the longest beach in the entire Basque Country. Unlike the tucked-away coves that define much of this rugged coastline, Zarautz's beach is wide, sandy, and open, backed by a long promenade lined with cafés, ice cream shops, and apartment blocks rather than cliffs. That combination of length, easy access, and a consistently reliable Atlantic swell is precisely what has made Zarautz one of Spain's most significant surf towns for close to sixty years, a reputation that shapes the town's identity far more than its modest year-round population would suggest.</p>
<h3>The Basque Country's Longest Beach</h3>
<p>At around 2.5 kilometres, Zarautz's beach is the longest continuous stretch of sand in the Basque Country, and its shape and orientation catch a reliable Atlantic swell that breaks consistently across a wide stretch of the bay. That makes it forgiving for beginners taking their first lessons in the shallower sections near town, while still offering enough power further out to challenge experienced surfers, which is a large part of why Zarautz has sustained a genuine surf culture rather than a passing one. Surf schools line the seafront promenade, board rental is easy to find in season, and the beach's width means swimmers, sunbathers, and surfers can share the same stretch of coast without constantly getting in each other's way, something narrower Basque beaches further along the coast cannot always manage.</p>
<h3>Where European Surfing Turned Professional</h3>
<p>Zarautz holds a specific place in European surfing history: it hosted the continent's first professional surf competition back in 1966, decades before surfing had any real foothold in mainstream Spanish or European sport. That early event set a precedent Zarautz has maintained ever since, and the town continues to host World Surf League competitions today, drawing professional surfers and a genuine competitive circuit back to the same stretch of sand nearly sixty years later. For visitors, that history shows up in more than plaques and old photographs; it's visible in the density of surf shops, board shapers, and schools along the seafront, and in a local surfing culture that treats the sport as an everyday part of town life rather than a tourist novelty bolted on for summer visitors. Travellers arriving outside competition dates will still find the same waves, the same schools, and a noticeably more relaxed, uncrowded version of the same scene.</p>
<h3>Beyond the Beach</h3>
<p>Zarautz is more than its surf reputation. The old town sits just back from the seafront, with narrow streets built around the Gothic church of Santa María la Real and a scattering of pintxo bars serving the same small-plate culture found across the wider Basque Country, typically paired with a glass of the crisp, slightly sparkling local white wine, txakoli, produced from vineyards visible on the hillsides just inland of town. A coastal stretch of the Camino de Santiago also passes directly through Zarautz, linking it to neighbouring towns for travellers interested in walking a section of the coast rather than driving it. The town's promenade, raised above the beach itself, gives a long, unbroken view along the whole curve of sand and out towards the headland, and it stays busy with walkers and cyclists well outside the main summer surf season.</p>
<h2>Planning Your Transfer</h2>
<p>Whether your visit to Zarautz is built around surfing, a relaxed beach stay, or a stop on a longer tour of the Basque coast, arranging your Bilbao Airport transfer in advance means one less thing to think about after a long flight. See our <a href="/bilbao-airport-transfer/">Bilbao Airport transfer</a> page for general details on arriving at BIO, and our airport transfers service if your itinerary includes other airports across the region.</p>`,
